      <floats-group id="article-22607.s15">
        <fig id="article-22607.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Intraventricular and Intraparenchymal Hemorrhage From Arteriovenous Malformations (AVM), Head CT. Contributed by S Dulebohn, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="avm" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22607.s16">
        <fig id="article-22607.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Subdural Hematoma Tentorium. Rupture of thin-walled bridging veins under opposing mechanical forces releases blood into the subdural space, resulting in a subdural hematoma. Contributed by S Dulebohn, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="SDH__tentorium" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22607.s17">
        <fig id="article-22607.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Brain Trauma Computed Tomography. This image shows a preoperative computed tomography (CT) scan of a patient with a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 14, demonstrating the following findings: Emergent CT imaging revealed a sagittally oriented skull fracture extending from the vertex to the foramen magnum, as well as a transverse parietal and temporal bone fracture. Multiple frontal, parietal, and temporal lobe contusions with associated interhemispheric hemorrhage and a left-sided subdural hematoma measuring 1.7 mm in greatest depth were appreciated. Effacement of the basilar cisterns was noted without shift of midline structures. T Rehman, R Ali, I Tawil, H Yonas, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brain_trauma_CT.jpg">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons&#x000a0;</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Brain_trauma_CT" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22607.s18">
        <fig id="article-22607.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>3D reconstructed DTI image on the left demonstrates marked bilateral frontal axonal thinning and fiber tract gaps in this patient with bilateral post-traumatic frontal contusions on conventional imaging. The image on right is a normal 3D DTI. Contributed by Travis Snyder DO, SimonMed Centers Las Vegas NV</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="DTI__image" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-22607.s19">
        <fig id="article-22607.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Original description: Figure 2. CT and enhanced gradient echo T2 star-weighted angiography (ESWAN) images of the brain of a 54-year-old man who experienced a traumatic brain injury. An axial head CT image displays right frontotemporal SAH (Fisher grade 4) with bilateral frontal contusions and intracerebral hematoma (A). A follow-up CT image 26 weeks after the brain injury indicates that the hemorrhages were completely resolved and the lateral ventricles were mildly enlarged (B). A follow-up MRI (1.5T) image was obtained 26 weeks following the head injury (C,D). The axial ESWAN image displays a rim of hypointensity (arrowheads), with hemosiderin deposits forming along the cerebral convexity (C, D). Contributed by Hongwei Zhao, Jin Wang, Zhonglie Lu, Qingjie Wu, Haijuan Lv, Hu Liu, Xiangyang Gong</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="CT_and_MRI_of_cerebral_hemorrhages_and_effective_T2_MRI_of_subsequent_hemosiderosis" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
